The exact offsets are unimportant, but if either the block source or destination offsets are negative, register BR1 will have bits 31 or 32 set (to signify the negative offset). This value is not cleared by hardware at the end of transfer, but the logic quoted in my original post checks that the entire register is zero to confirm the completion of a normal transfer.

I note that there are other areas of the HAL that do not work with 2D DMA (many functions overwrite the DMA data block length register will the full transfer size, rather than the block transfer size), so it may be that the HAL is not intended to be compatible with the 2D addressing modes. I did not see this documented however.

